{"message":"I hear that, and I don't want to dismiss the people you've talked to — but I'm not going to change my view based on a headcount. Consensus isn't evidence; plenty of widely-held beliefs turned out to be wrong. What would actually move me are arguments or data.

That said, I'll gladly steelman the other side: social media has connected isolated people, enabled social movements, democratized information access, and given small creators a livelihood. Those are real goods. My position is that the costs — mental health impacts especially on adolescents, algorithmic outrage amplification, erosion of shared epistemic ground — currently outweigh them.

If your friends or researchers have specific evidence I'm missing, I genuinely want to hear it. What's the strongest case they made?"}
